{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FRXfR3fD56cHmZ4YEdh8MZ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft — L2/036 ConvNextV2 Layer (NHWC persistence) Backward\n2\t\n3\tTarget HW: **NVIDIA A800, `sm_80` (Ampere)**. dtype: **float32** everywhere.\n4\tSubmission: `solution/solution.py` exposing `run(...)` with the exact signature from\n5\t`task/definition.json`. Primary compute must be **Triton**; PyTorch only for metadata,\n6\tallocation, and launch plumbing. **No Torch/CPU/NumPy/cuDNN-extension compute fallback.**\n7\t\n8\t> Skill applicability: `KernelWiki` targets Blackwell (sm_100) / Hopper (sm_90); `ncu-report-skill`\n9\t> targets B200 / sm_100. This task is Ampere (sm_80), so **neither skill applies**. No profiler /\n10\t> `nvidia-smi` / direct CUDA runs are permitted. Local ad-hoc execution (`Bash`) is denied in this\n11\t> environment, so **all functional feedback comes only from `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`**,\n12\t> each call costing one candidate evaluation against the 100-eval budget.\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## 1. What the operation is\n17\t\n18\tThis is the **backward pass** of one ConvNextV2 block, written with NHWC \"persistence\" (activations\n19\tkept in `(B,H,W,C)` between stages). All forward intermediates are **provided as inputs** (saved from\n20\tthe forward), so we never recompute the forward — we only propagate gradients in reverse order and emit\n21\t11 gradient tensors.\n22\t\n23\tConstants: `C = 128` (channels), `C4 = 4*C = 512` (expanded channels), depthwise kernel `7x7`,\n24\t`padding = 3`, `groups = C` (true depthwise), `eps = 1e-6`, `drop_path_prob = 0.1`\n25\t(so `keep_prob = 0.9`, drop-path branch is always active in these workloads).\n26\t\n27\tForward block (for context; **not** recomputed):\n28\t`residual → dwconv(7x7,dw) → permute NCHW→NHWC → LayerNorm(affine) → pwconv1 (C→C4) → GELU(tanh) →\n29\tGRN → pwconv2 (C4→C) → permute NHWC→NCHW → drop_path → + residual`.\n30\t\n31\t### 1.1 Notation / shapes\n32\tLet `M = B*H*W` be the number of spatial tokens (flattened `(B,H,W)`), `HW = H*W`.\n33\t\n34\t| tensor | shape | role |\n35\t|---|---|---|\n36\t| grad_output, residual, x_dwconv | (B,C,H,W) NCHW | upstream grad / saved inputs |\n37\t| x_nhwc, x_normalized, x_ln | (B,H,W,C) | LN activations |\n38\t| mean, var | (B,H,W,1) | LN stats (per token, over C) |\n39\t| x_expanded, x_gelu, x_grn_scaled, x_grn | (B,H,W,C4) | pwconv1/GELU/GRN activations |\n40\t| global_features, norm_features | (B,1,1,C4) | GRN spatial L2 norm / normalized |\n41\t| gf_mean | (B,1,1,1) | channel mean of global_features |\n42\t| dwconv_weight | (C,1,7,7) | depthwise kernel |\n43\t| layernorm_weight | (C,) | LN gamma |\n44\t| pwconv1_weight | (C4,C) | expansion matrix |\n45\t| grn_weight | (1,1,1,C4) | GRN gamma |\n46\t| pwconv2_weight | (C,C4) | projection matrix |\n47\t| drop_mask | (B,1,1,1) | per-sample keep mask |\n48\t\n49\tOutputs (11): `grad_x (B,C,H,W)`, `grad_dwconv_weight (C,1,7,7)`, `grad_dwconv_bias (C,)`,\n50\t`grad_layernorm_weight (C,)`, `grad_layernorm_bias (C,)`, `grad_pwconv1_weight (C4,C)`,\n51\t`grad_pwconv1_bias (C4,)`, `grad_grn_weight (1,1,1,C4)`, `grad_grn_bias (1,1,1,C4)`,\n52\t`grad_pwconv2_weight (C,C4)`, `grad_pwconv2_bias (C,)`.\n53\t\n54\t### 1.2 Feedback workloads (fixed 5)\n55\t| id | B | H | W | M=B·H·W | HW | atol | rtol | match |\n56\t|----|---|---|---|--------|------|------|------|-------|\n57\t| W1 | 2 | 28 | 28 | 1,568  | 784  | 0.68 | 1e-3 | 0.98 |\n58\t| W2 | 1 | 56 | 56 | 3,136  | 3136 | 0.75 | 1e-3 | 0.98 |\n59\t| W3 | 16| 56 | 56 | 50,176 | 3136 | 5.2  | 1e-3 | 0.98 |\n60\t| W4 | 16| 14 | 14 | 3,136  | 196  | 0.96 | 1e-3 | 0.98 |\n61\t| W5 | 4 | 56 | 56 | 12,544 | 3136 | 1.3  | 1e-3 | 0.98 |\n62\t\n63\tW3 is by far the largest (dominates the geomean cost); W1/W4 are small (latency/launch-overhead bound).\n64\t`atol` grows with problem size (`grad_dwconv_weight` and other reductions sum over `M`, so their\n65\tmagnitude scales with `M` — the evaluator widens `atol` accordingly).\n66\t\n67\t---\n68\t\n69\t## 2. Exact reference math to replicate (bit-for-bit intent)\n70\t\n71\tThe evaluator compares against `run(...)` in `definition.json`. We must reproduce its **exact\n72\tarithmetic**, including one non-standard chain-rule step, not the \"textbook\" gradient. Reverse order:\n73\t\n74\t1. **Residual + drop-path split.** `grad_residual = grad_output` (raw, *no* drop-path scaling).\n75\t   `grad_x_nchw = grad_output * drop_mask / keep_prob` (`keep_prob = 1 - drop_path_prob = 0.9`).\n76\t2. **Permute NCHW→NHWC.** `grad_x_projected[b,h,w,c] = grad_x_nchw[b,c,h,w]` → `(B,H,W,C)`.\n77\t3. **pwconv2 backward.** `F.linear(x, pwconv2_weight.t()) == x @ pwconv2_weight`.\n78\t   - `grad_x_grn = grad_x_projected @ pwconv2_weight`  → `(M,C)·(C,C4)=(M,C4)`.\n79\t   - `grad_pwconv2_weight = grad_x_projected_flatᵀ @ x_grn_flat` → `(C,M)·(M,C4)=(C,C4)`.\n80\t   - `grad_pwconv2_bias = grad_x_projected.sum(0,1,2)` → `(C,)`.\n81\t4. **GRN backward.** `x_grn = grn_weight * x_grn_scaled + x_gelu` (bias≡0 in fwd but grad still emitted);\n82\t   `x_grn_scaled = x_gelu * norm_features` (norm_features broadcast over H,W).\n83\t   - `grad_x_grn_scaled = grad_x_grn * grn_weight`.\n84\t   - `grad_grn_weight = (grad_x_grn * x_grn_scaled).sum(0,1,2, keepdim)` → `(1,1,1,C4)`.\n85\t   - `grad_grn_bias = grad_x_grn.sum(0,1,2, keepdim)` → `(1,1,1,C4)`.\n86\t   - `grad_norm_features = (grad_x_grn_scaled * x_gelu).sum(1,2, keepdim)` → `(B,1,1,C4)` (**spatial** reduction per (b,c)).\n87\t   - `grad_x_gelu = grad_x_grn (residual path) + grad_x_grn_scaled * norm_features (scaled path)`.\n88\t5. **norm_features = global_features / (gf_mean + eps).**\n89\t   - `grad_global_features = grad_norm_features / (gf_mean + eps)`.\n90\t   - `grad_gf_mean = -grad_norm_features * global_features / (gf_mean + eps)**2`  → kept **element-wise `(B,1,1,C4)`, NOT summed**.\n91\t   - `grad_global_features += grad_gf_mean / C4` (added element-wise, channel `c` gets only its own term).\n92\t   - ⚠️ **Non-standard**: the true gradient of `gf_mean = mean_c(global_features)` would sum\n93\t     `grad_gf_mean` over channels and broadcast `/C4`. The reference does **not** sum. We must copy this\n94\t     exactly (per-(b,c): `ggf[b,c] = grad_nf[b,c]/(gfm[b]+eps) - grad_nf[b,c]*gf[b,c]/((gfm[b]+eps)²·C4)`).\n95\t6. **global_features = ‖x_gelu‖₂ over spatial (dims 1,2).**\n96\t   `grad_x_gelu += x_gelu * grad_global_features / (global_features + eps)` (broadcast gf/ggf over H,W).\n97\t7. **GELU backward (tanh approx).** Constants `k0 = 0.7978845608028654 = sqrt(2/π)`, `k1 = 0.044715`.\n98\t   `inner = k0*(x_expanded + k1*x_expanded³)`; `t = tanh(inner)`; `cdf = 0.5*(1+t)`;\n99\t   `pdf = 0.5*(1-t²)*k0*(1 + 3*k1*x_expanded²)`; `gelu_grad = cdf + x_expanded*pdf`;\n100\t   `grad_x_expanded = grad_x_gelu * gelu_grad`. (Use `x_expanded` given; do not re-derive from x_gelu.)\n101\t8. **pwconv1 backward.** `F.linear(x, pwconv1_weight.t()) == x @ pwconv1_weight`.\n102\t   - `grad_x_ln = grad_x_expanded @ pwconv1_weight` → `(M,C4)·(C4,C)=(M,C)`.\n103\t   - `grad_pwconv1_weight = grad_x_expanded_flatᵀ @ x_ln_flat` → `(C4,M)·(M,C)=(C4,C)`.\n104\t   - `grad_pwconv1_bias = grad_x_expanded.sum(0,1,2)` → `(C4,)`.\n105\t9. **LayerNorm affine backward.**\n106\t   - `grad_x_normalized = grad_x_ln * layernorm_weight`.\n107\t   - `grad_layernorm_weight = (grad_x_ln * x_normalized).sum(0,1,2)` → `(C,)`.\n108\t   - `grad_layernorm_bias = grad_x_ln.sum(0,1,2)` → `(C,)`.\n109\t10. **LayerNorm normalization backward** (`N = C = 128`, reduction over channel axis per token):\n110\t    - `std = sqrt(var + eps)` (use given `var`).\n111\t    - `grad_x_nhwc = grad_x_normalized / std`\n112\t    - `grad_var = -(grad_x_normalized*(x_nhwc-mean)).sum(-1,keepdim) / (2*(var+eps)*std)`\n113\t    - `grad_mean = -(grad_x_normalized/std).sum(-1,keepdim)`\n114\t    - `grad_mean += grad_var * (-2*(x_nhwc-mean).sum(-1,keepdim)/N)`  (this `(x-mean).sum` ≈ 0 numerically but MUST be kept)\n115\t    - `grad_x_nhwc += grad_var * (2*(x_nhwc-mean)/N)`\n116\t    - `grad_x_nhwc += grad_mean / N`\n117\t11. **Permute NHWC→NCHW.** `grad_x_dwconv[b,c,h,w] = grad_x_nhwc[b,h,w,c]`.\n118\t12. **Depthwise conv backward** (kernel 7x7, pad 3, stride 1, groups C):\n119\t    - **Input grad**: `grad_x = conv_transpose2d(grad_x_dwconv, dwconv_weight, pad=3, groups=C) + grad_residual`.\n120\t      Explicit stencil: `grad_x[b,c,iy,ix] = grad_output[b,c,iy,ix] + Σ_{i,j∈0..6} grad_x_dwconv[b,c, iy-i+3, ix-j+3]·W[c,0,i,j]`\n121\t      (valid taps only, i.e. `0 ≤ iy-i+3 < H`, `0 ≤ ix-j+3 < W`). Note the **flip** of taps vs forward.\n122\t    - **Weight grad**: `grad_dwconv_weight[c,0,i,j] = Σ_{b,oy,ox} grad_x_dwconv[b,c,oy,ox] · residual_pad[b,c, oy+i, ox+j]`,\n123\t      `residual_pad` = residual zero-padded by 3. Equivalent: `Σ grad·residual[b,c, oy+i-3, ox+j-3]` over valid positions.\n124\t    - **Bias grad**: `grad_dwconv_bias[c] = grad_x_dwconv.sum(0,2,3)` → `(C,)`.\n125\t\n126\t**Cross-check of the two conv directions (off-by-one is the top functional risk):** forward uses\n127\t`out[oy,ox] = Σ in_pad[oy+i, ox+j]·W[i,j]` (cross-correlation, pad 3). Therefore input-grad uses the\n128\tflipped kernel and weight-grad correlates grad with the padded input at offset `(+i,+j)`. Both must be\n129\tvalidated by construction and, if a candidate fails only on `grad_x` / `grad_dwconv_weight`, this\n130\tindexing is the first suspect.\n131\t\n132\t---\n133\t\n134\t## 3. Constraints & correctness invariants\n135\t\n136\t- **Triton-only compute.** Every one of: 4 GEMMs (pwconv1/2 fwd-grad + weight-grad), all element-wise\n137\t  chains, all reductions (biases, LN weight/bias, GRN weight/bias, spatial L2-norm grad, LN stat grads),\n138\t  and both depthwise-conv gradients must be Triton. Torch may only `empty`/`zeros`/`reshape`/`view`/\n139\t  `stride`/`permute-metadata` and launch kernels.\n140\t- **No fallback.** A failing Triton path is invalid; do not substitute `F.linear`/`F.conv*`/`torch.tanh`.\n141\t- **Exact arithmetic** for the non-standard `gf_mean` step and the `(x-mean).sum` LN term (§2.5, §2.10).\n142\t- **Use provided intermediates** (`mean`, `var`, `global_features`, `gf_mean`, `norm_features`,\n143\t  `x_grn_scaled`, `x_normalized`, `x_ln`, `x_expanded`, `x_gelu`) directly — do not recompute from\n144\t  scratch, or rounding will drift from the reference.\n145\t- **Output dtypes/shapes** must match exactly, including keep-dim shapes `grad_grn_weight/bias (1,1,1,C4)`\n146\t  and `grad_pwconv2_bias (C,)`.\n147\t- **Immutability**: one source version per candidate id; new id for any source/config/launch change.\n148\t\n149\t---\n150\t\n151\t## 4. Numerical risk register\n152\t\n153\t1. **TF32 vs IEEE fp32 in `tl.dot`.** `rtol = 1e-3` sits right at TF32's (~2⁻¹⁰ mantissa) error edge.\n154\t   Accumulation is fp32 regardless, but TF32 truncates multiply operands. For large-magnitude reduction\n155\t   outputs the wide `atol` likely absorbs it, but `grad_x` / LN-derived grads have modest magnitude where\n156\t   `rtol` bites. **Plan:** start with `input_precision=\"ieee\"` (true fp32) for all `tl.dot` to guarantee\n157\t   correctness; later test `\"tf32\"` as a *speed* candidate and keep it only if all 5 workloads still pass.\n158\t2. **Non-standard `gf_mean` gradient (§2.5).** Copying the textbook (summed) version will fail the\n159\t   `grad_grn_*` / `grad_x` chain. Replicate the element-wise, unsummed form exactly.\n160\t3. **GELU tanh approx.** Reuse the exact constants and the *single* `tanh` evaluation reused in `cdf`\n161\t   and `pdf`; recomputing/reordering changes low bits. Use `tl.math.tanh` (or `libdevice.tanh`) in fp32.\n162\t4. **Divisions by (stat+eps).** `1/std`, `1/(gf_mean+eps)`, `1/(global_features+eps)`. `global_features`\n163\t   is an L2 norm ≥ 0; `+eps` guards zero. Use given values, not recomputed norms.\n164\t5. **Depthwise stencil boundaries (pad 3).** Off-by-one / flip errors in input-grad and weight-grad are\n165\t   the highest-probability functional bug (see §2.12 cross-check). Mask out-of-range taps to 0.\n166\t6. **Reduction associativity / atomics.** `grad_dwconv_weight`, biases, LN/GRN weight&bias sum over many\n167\t   elements. Atomic-add or multi-block split changes summation order → last-bit noise, acceptable under\n168\t   `atol`/`rtol`+98% match, but prefer deterministic (single-pass or tree) reductions where cheap to keep\n169\t   headroom. Accumulate in fp32.\n170\t7. **Match ratio 0.98.** Up to 2% of elements per tensor may miss; still aim for ~exact so no tensor\n171\t   drifts systematically (a wrong constant would fail *all* elements, which 0.98 does not save).\n172\t8. **Large-M accumulation error** (W3, M≈50k) for `grad_pwconv*_weight` (K=M reduction). fp32 accumulate;\n173\t   consider split-K with fp32 partials if a single accumulator loses precision (unlikely to matter given\n174\t   atol 5.2 on W3).\n175\t9. **Empty/degenerate tiles**: masks for M not divisible by BLOCK, C4=512/C=128 are clean multiples of\n176\t   common block sizes (32/64/128), which simplifies masking on the channel axes.\n177\t\n178\t---\n179\t\n180\t## 5. Where the reference is slow (optimization opportunity)\n181\t\n182\tThe reference `run(...)` has one catastrophic hotspot and several ordinary ops:\n183\t\n184\t- 🔴 **`for g in range(C): ...unfold...`** — a **Python loop over 128 channels**, each doing\n185\t  `F.pad` + double `unfold` (materializing `(B,H,W,7,7)` patches) + multiply + 3-D sum. This is the\n186\t  dominant cost, especially on W3 (128 × huge unfold), and launch/host overhead on small W1/W4. A single\n187\t  fused Triton depthwise-weight-grad kernel replaces all 128 iterations → the primary speedup lever.\n188\t- 🟠 4 GEMMs via cuBLAS (fast baseline; our Triton GEMM must be competitive, not just correct).\n189\t- 🟠 `conv_transpose2d` via cuDNN for `grad_x` (fast baseline; depthwise stencil in Triton must match).\n190\t- 🟢 Many element-wise/reduction ops materializing full `(M,C4)` temporaries repeatedly → **fusion**\n191\t  reduces DRAM traffic (memory-bound).\n192\t\n193\t**Thesis:** even a *modular, correct* Triton port should beat the reference purely by eliminating the\n194\t128-iteration Python loop; further gains come from (a) a well-tuned depthwise weight-grad kernel and\n195\t(b) fusing the element-wise chains into the GEMM epilogues / stencil prologues.\n196\t\n197\t---\n198\t\n199\t## 6. Triton design space\n200\t\n201\tPipeline temporaries kept in **NHWC-flat `(M, ·)`** contiguous buffers so GEMMs are simple row-major:\n202\t`grad_x_projected (M,C)`, `grad_x_grn (M,C4)`, `grad_x_expanded (M,C4)`, `grad_x_ln (M,C)`,\n203\t`grad_x_nhwc (M,C)`, `grad_x_dwconv (B,C,H,W)`.\n204\t\n205\t### 6.1 Kernel decomposition (baseline modular plan for c001)\n206\t- **K1 — permute+drop-path+bias** (grid over (M tiles, C)): compute `grad_x_projected[b,h,w,c] =\n207\t  grad_output[b,c,h,w]*drop_mask[b]/keep_prob`; accumulate `grad_pwconv2_bias[c]` (reduction over M).\n208\t- **K2 — GEMM** `grad_x_grn = grad_x_projected @ pwconv2_weight` (M×C×C4), fp32 `tl.dot`.\n209\t- **K3 — GEMM** `grad_pwconv2_weight = grad_x_projectedᵀ @ x_grn` (C×C4, K=M).\n210\t- **K4 — GRN reduce (pass 1)**: per (b,c) spatial sums → `grad_norm_features (B,C4)`; global sums →\n211\t  `grad_grn_weight (C4)`, `grad_grn_bias (C4)`. Then compute `grad_global_features (B,C4)` from\n212\t  `grad_norm_features`, `gf_mean`, `global_features` (§2.5, non-standard).\n213\t- **K5 — GRN apply + GELU (pass 2)**: `grad_x_gelu` (residual + scaled + L2-norm paths) then GELU-grad →\n214\t  `grad_x_expanded (M,C4)`.\n215\t- **K6 — GEMM** `grad_x_ln = grad_x_expanded @ pwconv1_weight` (M×C4×C).\n216\t- **K7 — GEMM** `grad_pwconv1_weight = grad_x_expandedᵀ @ x_ln` (C4×C, K=M); `grad_pwconv1_bias (C4)`\n217\t  (reduce over M — can be epilogue of K5 or a small reduce).\n218\t- **K8 — LN affine + normalization backward**: per-token (row) load C=128 channels, compute\n219\t  `grad_layernorm_weight/bias` (reduce over M, accumulate) and `grad_x_nhwc (M,C)` via §2.9–2.10.\n220\t  C=128 fits one row-block → single-pass row kernel (like Triton LN-bwd).\n221\t- **K9 — depthwise input-grad stencil**: `grad_x = stencil(grad_x_nhwc→NCHW, W_flip) + grad_output`.\n222\t- **K10 — depthwise weight-grad + bias**: `grad_dwconv_weight (C,49)` and `grad_dwconv_bias (C)` via\n223\t  correlation of `grad_x_dwconv` with padded `residual`.\n224\t\n225\t### 6.2 Depthwise weight-grad options (highest-value kernel)\n226\t- (a) **One program per channel**, serial grid-stride over `B×HW`, 49 fp32 register accumulators.\n227\t  Simplest/deterministic; risk: single program serializes all M for a channel (W3 heavy).\n228\t- (b) **Split over (channel, spatial-block)**, each program accumulates 49 partials for its block, then\n229\t  `atomic_add` into `grad_dwconv_weight[c]` (or a `(nblk,C,49)` scratch + reduce kernel). Better\n230\t  parallelism for W3; atomics add nondeterminism (tolerable).\n231\t- (c) **Tile over channels in the inner axis** (vectorized 49-tap over a channel block) to raise ILP.\n232\t  Start with (a) for c001 correctness; move to (b)/(c) for W3 throughput.\n233\t\n234\t### 6.3 Depthwise input-grad options\n235\t- Per-`(b, c-block, spatial-tile)` stencil, 49 taps of flipped weight, boundary-masked, `+ grad_output`.\n236\t  Channels contiguous (NCHW) → coalesced along W. Consider caching the 49 weights in registers/shared.\n237\t\n238\t### 6.4 GEMM tuning axes\n239\t- Block sizes `(BM, BN, BK)`, `num_warps ∈ {4,8}`, `num_stages ∈ {2,3,4}`, `input_precision`\n240\t  (`ieee` first, `tf32` later), grouped-M ordering for L2 reuse. K is 128/512/M.\n241\t- **Fusion candidates**: fold K1's drop-path/permute into K2's A-load; fold `grad_pwconv2_bias` /\n242\t  `grad_pwconv1_bias` into GEMM epilogues; fuse K4/K5 GELU into K6's A-load; fuse LN-affine into K8.\n243\t  Autotune once shapes are known; C4=512, C=128 are TensorCore-friendly multiples.\n244\t\n245\t### 6.5 Persistence / occupancy\n246\t- Reuse `(M,C4)` buffers across stages to cap DRAM. Small workloads (W1/W4) are launch-bound → minimize\n247\t  kernel count / prefer fused kernels there; large W3 is compute/BW-bound → prioritize GEMM + weight-grad\n248\t  throughput. A single set of launch params must serve all 5 (immutable per candidate), so autotune keys\n249\t  on shape.\n250\t\n251\t---\n252\t\n253\t## 7. Implementation & candidate strategy (for plan.md later)\n254\t\n255\t- **c001 — correctness-first modular port.** All 10 kernels above, `input_precision=\"ieee\"`, deterministic\n256\t  reductions, conservative block sizes. Goal: pass all 5 workloads; likely already > reference (kills the\n257\t  Python loop). Establishes the correctness baseline and per-tensor sanity.\n258\t- **c002+ — targeted speed** once green: (i) depthwise weight-grad option (b) split+atomic for W3;\n259\t  (ii) GEMM autotune / grouped-M; (iii) fuse element-wise chains into GEMM epilogues & stencil prologues;\n260\t  (iv) try `tf32` (guard: must keep all 5 passing); (v) reduce kernel count for W1/W4 launch overhead.\n261\t- Change **one lever per candidate id**; re-evaluate; keep only monotone geomean improvements; record\n262\t  parent/hash/hypothesis/per-workload/decision in `candidates.jsonl`.\n263\t\n264\t## 8. Validation strategy\n265\t\n266\t- **No local execution available** (Bash denied; no CUDA/profiler/nvidia-smi allowed). The *only* trusted\n267\t  signal is `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` (5 workloads = 1 eval). Budget: 100 evals,\n268\t  tokens soft 1.0M / hard 1.2M — so **spend evals deliberately**, not as a debugger.\n269\t- **Pre-eval self-checks (by construction):** (1) shape/stride table for every kernel I/O matches §1.1\n270\t  outputs; (2) reverse-order math matches §2 line-by-line, including the two non-standard steps; (3)\n271\t  depthwise flip/offset cross-check (§2.12); (4) all `tl.dot` accumulate fp32; (5) no Torch compute path.\n272\t- **Failure triage plan:** the evaluator reports per-workload pass/fail (and, we expect, which output\n273\t  mismatches). Map failures to suspects — `grad_x`/`grad_dwconv_weight` → conv indexing; `grad_grn_*`/\n274\t  `grad_x_gelu` → §2.5 non-standard step; broad small-magnitude misses on many tensors → TF32; single\n275\t  tensor all-fail → a wrong constant/transpose.\n276\t- **Metric:** geometric-mean speedup across the 5 feedback workloads, gated on every workload passing\n277\t  correctness. Final 14-workload eval is operator-approval-only; never auto-run `final`.\n278\t- **Convergence / stop:** stop when geomean plateaus across successive candidates, or on budget; then\n279\t  write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with the reason.\n280\t\n281\t## 9. Open questions to resolve during implementation\n282\t- Exact evaluator match rule (assumed `|a-b| ≤ atol + rtol·|ref|`, ≥98% elements). Confirm empirically on c001.\n283\t- Whether `tf32` passes `rtol=1e-3` on the small workloads (W1/W2/W4) — test as a dedicated candidate.\n284\t- Best depthwise weight-grad parallelization for the wide range M∈[1568, 50176] under one immutable config.\n285\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"be1a2ffd-3ba4-41ee-9e28-6af46e47a563","uuid":"15ab1809-b4e0-46cf-a069-20414974d0be","timestamp":"2026-09-16T18:00:14.720Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-036_convnextv2_layer_with_nhwc_persistence_backward/docs/draft.md","content":"# Draft — L2/036 ConvNextV2 Layer (NHWC persistence) Backward\n\nTarget HW: **NVIDIA A800, `sm_80` (Ampere)**. dtype: **float32** everywhere.\nSubmission: `solution/solution.py` exposing `run(...)` with the exact signature from\n`task/definition.json`. Primary compute must be **Triton**; PyTorch only for metadata,\nallocation, and launch plumbing. **No Torch/CPU/NumPy/cuDNN-extension compute fallback.**\n\n> Skill applicability: `KernelWiki` targets Blackwell (sm_100) / Hopper (sm_90); `ncu-report-skill`\n> targets B200 / sm_100. This task is Ampere (sm_80), so **neither skill applies**. No profiler /\n> `nvidia-smi` / direct CUDA runs are permitted. Local ad-hoc execution (`Bash`) is denied in this\n> environment, so **all functional feedback comes only from `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`**,\n> each call costing one candidate evaluation against the 100-eval budget.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the operation is\n\nThis is the **backward pass** of one ConvNextV2 block, written with NHWC \"persistence\" (activations\nkept in `(B,H,W,C)` between stages). All forward intermediates are **provided as inputs** (saved from\nthe forward), so we never recompute the forward — we only propagate gradients in reverse order and emit\n11 gradient tensors.\n\nConstants: `C = 128` (channels), `C4 = 4*C = 512` (expanded channels), depthwise kernel `7x7`,\n`padding = 3`, `groups = C` (true depthwise), `eps = 1e-6`, `drop_path_prob = 0.1`\n(so `keep_prob = 0.9`, drop-path branch is always active in these workloads).\n\nForward block (for context; **not** recomputed):\n`residual → dwconv(7x7,dw) → permute NCHW→NHWC → LayerNorm(affine) → pwconv1 (C→C4) → GELU(tanh) →\nGRN → pwconv2 (C4→C) → permute NHWC→NCHW → drop_path → + residual`.\n\n### 1.1 Notation / shapes\nLet `M = B*H*W` be the number of spatial tokens (flattened `(B,H,W)`), `HW = H*W`.\n\n| tensor | shape | role |\n|---|---|---|\n| grad_output, residual, x_dwconv | (B,C,H,W) NCHW | upstream grad / saved inputs |\n| x_nhwc, x_normalized, x_ln | (B,H,W,C) | LN activations |\n| mean, var | (B,H,W,1) | LN stats (per token, over C) |\n| x_expanded, x_gelu, x_grn_scaled, x_grn | (B,H,W,C4) | pwconv1/GELU/GRN activations |\n| global_features, norm_features | (B,1,1,C4) | GRN spatial L2 norm / normalized |\n| gf_mean | (B,1,1,1) | channel mean of global_features |\n| dwconv_weight | (C,1,7,7) | depthwise kernel |\n| layernorm_weight | (C,) | LN gamma |\n| pwconv1_weight | (C4,C) | expansion matrix |\n| grn_weight | (1,1,1,C4) | GRN gamma |\n| pwconv2_weight | (C,C4) | projection matrix |\n| drop_mask | (B,1,1,1) | per-sample keep mask |\n\nOutputs (11): `grad_x (B,C,H,W)`, `grad_dwconv_weight (C,1,7,7)`, `grad_dwconv_bias (C,)`,\n`grad_layernorm_weight (C,)`, `grad_layernorm_bias (C,)`, `grad_pwconv1_weight (C4,C)`,\n`grad_pwconv1_bias (C4,)`, `grad_grn_weight (1,1,1,C4)`, `grad_grn_bias (1,1,1,C4)`,\n`grad_pwconv2_weight (C,C4)`, `grad_pwconv2_bias (C,)`.\n\n### 1.2 Feedback workloads (fixed 5)\n| id | B | H | W | M=B·H·W | HW | atol | rtol | match |\n|----|---|---|---|--------|------|------|------|-------|\n| W1 | 2 | 28 | 28 | 1,568  | 784  | 0.68 | 1e-3 | 0.98 |\n| W2 | 1 | 56 | 56 | 3,136  | 3136 | 0.75 | 1e-3 | 0.98 |\n| W3 | 16| 56 | 56 | 50,176 | 3136 | 5.2  | 1e-3 | 0.98 |\n| W4 | 16| 14 | 14 | 3,136  | 196  | 0.96 | 1e-3 | 0.98 |\n| W5 | 4 | 56 | 56 | 12,544 | 3136 | 1.3  | 1e-3 | 0.98 |\n\nW3 is by far the largest (dominates the geomean cost); W1/W4 are small (latency/launch-overhead bound).\n`atol` grows with problem size (`grad_dwconv_weight` and other reductions sum over `M`, so their\nmagnitude scales with `M` — the evaluator widens `atol` accordingly).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Exact reference math to replicate (bit-for-bit intent)\n\nThe evaluator compares against `run(...)` in `definition.json`. We must reproduce its **exact\narithmetic**, including one non-standard chain-rule step, not the \"textbook\" gradient. Reverse order:\n\n1. **Residual + drop-path split.** `grad_residual = grad_output` (raw, *no* drop-path scaling).\n   `grad_x_nchw = grad_output * drop_mask / keep_prob` (`keep_prob = 1 - drop_path_prob = 0.9`).\n2. **Permute NCHW→NHWC.** `grad_x_projected[b,h,w,c] = grad_x_nchw[b,c,h,w]` → `(B,H,W,C)`.\n3. **pwconv2 backward.** `F.linear(x, pwconv2_weight.t()) == x @ pwconv2_weight`.\n   - `grad_x_grn = grad_x_projected @ pwconv2_weight`  → `(M,C)·(C,C4)=(M,C4)`.\n   - `grad_pwconv2_weight = grad_x_projected_flatᵀ @ x_grn_flat` → `(C,M)·(M,C4)=(C,C4)`.\n   - `grad_pwconv2_bias = grad_x_projected.sum(0,1,2)` → `(C,)`.\n4. **GRN backward.** `x_grn = grn_weight * x_grn_scaled + x_gelu` (bias≡0 in fwd but grad still emitted);\n   `x_grn_scaled = x_gelu * norm_features` (norm_features broadcast over H,W).\n   - `grad_x_grn_scaled = grad_x_grn * grn_weight`.\n   - `grad_grn_weight = (grad_x_grn * x_grn_scaled).sum(0,1,2, keepdim)` → `(1,1,1,C4)`.\n   - `grad_grn_bias = grad_x_grn.sum(0,1,2, keepdim)` → `(1,1,1,C4)`.\n   - `grad_norm_features = (grad_x_grn_scaled * x_gelu).sum(1,2, keepdim)` → `(B,1,1,C4)` (**spatial** reduction per (b,c)).\n   - `grad_x_gelu = grad_x_grn (residual path) + grad_x_grn_scaled * norm_features (scaled path)`.\n5. **norm_features = global_features / (gf_mean + eps).**\n   - `grad_global_features = grad_norm_features / (gf_mean + eps)`.\n   - `grad_gf_mean = -grad_norm_features * global_features / (gf_mean + eps)**2`  → kept **element-wise `(B,1,1,C4)`, NOT summed**.\n   - `grad_global_features += grad_gf_mean / C4` (added element-wise, channel `c` gets only its own term).\n   - ⚠️ **Non-standard**: the true gradient of `gf_mean = mean_c(global_features)` would sum\n     `grad_gf_mean` over channels and broadcast `/C4`. The reference does **not** sum. We must copy this\n     exactly (per-(b,c): `ggf[b,c] = grad_nf[b,c]/(gfm[b]+eps) - grad_nf[b,c]*gf[b,c]/((gfm[b]+eps)²·C4)`).\n6. **global_features = ‖x_gelu‖₂ over spatial (dims 1,2).**\n   `grad_x_gelu += x_gelu * grad_global_features / (global_features + eps)` (broadcast gf/ggf over H,W).\n7. **GELU backward (tanh approx).** Constants `k0 = 0.7978845608028654 = sqrt(2/π)`, `k1 = 0.044715`.\n   `inner = k0*(x_expanded + k1*x_expanded³)`; `t = tanh(inner)`; `cdf = 0.5*(1+t)`;\n   `pdf = 0.5*(1-t²)*k0*(1 + 3*k1*x_expanded²)`; `gelu_grad = cdf + x_expanded*pdf`;\n   `grad_x_expanded = grad_x_gelu * gelu_grad`. (Use `x_expanded` given; do not re-derive from x_gelu.)\n8. **pwconv1 backward.** `F.linear(x, pwconv1_weight.t()) == x @ pwconv1_weight`.\n   - `grad_x_ln = grad_x_expanded @ pwconv1_weight` → `(M,C4)·(C4,C)=(M,C)`.\n   - `grad_pwconv1_weight = grad_x_expanded_flatᵀ @ x_ln_flat` → `(C4,M)·(M,C)=(C4,C)`.\n   - `grad_pwconv1_bias = grad_x_expanded.sum(0,1,2)` → `(C4,)`.\n9. **LayerNorm affine backward.**\n   - `grad_x_normalized = grad_x_ln * layernorm_weight`.\n   - `grad_layernorm_weight = (grad_x_ln * x_normalized).sum(0,1,2)` → `(C,)`.\n   - `grad_layernorm_bias = grad_x_ln.sum(0,1,2)` → `(C,)`.\n10. **LayerNorm normalization backward** (`N = C = 128`, reduction over channel axis per token):\n    - `std = sqrt(var + eps)` (use given `var`).\n    - `grad_x_nhwc = grad_x_normalized / std`\n    - `grad_var = -(grad_x_normalized*(x_nhwc-mean)).sum(-1,keepdim) / (2*(var+eps)*std)`\n    - `grad_mean = -(grad_x_normalized/std).sum(-1,keepdim)`\n    - `grad_mean += grad_var * (-2*(x_nhwc-mean).sum(-1,keepdim)/N)`  (this `(x-mean).sum` ≈ 0 numerically but MUST be kept)\n    - `grad_x_nhwc += grad_var * (2*(x_nhwc-mean)/N)`\n    - `grad_x_nhwc += grad_mean / N`\n11. **Permute NHWC→NCHW.** `grad_x_dwconv[b,c,h,w] = grad_x_nhwc[b,h,w,c]`.\n12. **Depthwise conv backward** (kernel 7x7, pad 3, stride 1, groups C):\n    - **Input grad**: `grad_x = conv_transpose2d(grad_x_dwconv, dwconv_weight, pad=3, groups=C) + grad_residual`.\n      Explicit stencil: `grad_x[b,c,iy,ix] = grad_output[b,c,iy,ix] + Σ_{i,j∈0..6} grad_x_dwconv[b,c, iy-i+3, ix-j+3]·W[c,0,i,j]`\n      (valid taps only, i.e. `0 ≤ iy-i+3 < H`, `0 ≤ ix-j+3 < W`). Note the **flip** of taps vs forward.\n    - **Weight grad**: `grad_dwconv_weight[c,0,i,j] = Σ_{b,oy,ox} grad_x_dwconv[b,c,oy,ox] · residual_pad[b,c, oy+i, ox+j]`,\n      `residual_pad` = residual zero-padded by 3. Equivalent: `Σ grad·residual[b,c, oy+i-3, ox+j-3]` over valid positions.\n    - **Bias grad**: `grad_dwconv_bias[c] = grad_x_dwconv.sum(0,2,3)` → `(C,)`.\n\n**Cross-check of the two conv directions (off-by-one is the top functional risk):** forward uses\n`out[oy,ox] = Σ in_pad[oy+i, ox+j]·W[i,j]` (cross-correlation, pad 3). Therefore input-grad uses the\nflipped kernel and weight-grad correlates grad with the padded input at offset `(+i,+j)`. Both must be\nvalidated by construction and, if a candidate fails only on `grad_x` / `grad_dwconv_weight`, this\nindexing is the first suspect.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Constraints & correctness invariants\n\n- **Triton-only compute.** Every one of: 4 GEMMs (pwconv1/2 fwd-grad + weight-grad), all element-wise\n  chains, all reductions (biases, LN weight/bias, GRN weight/bias, spatial L2-norm grad, LN stat grads),\n  and both depthwise-conv gradients must be Triton. Torch may only `empty`/`zeros`/`reshape`/`view`/\n  `stride`/`permute-metadata` and launch kernels.\n- **No fallback.** A failing Triton path is invalid; do not substitute `F.linear`/`F.conv*`/`torch.tanh`.\n- **Exact arithmetic** for the non-standard `gf_mean` step and the `(x-mean).sum` LN term (§2.5, §2.10).\n- **Use provided intermediates** (`mean`, `var`, `global_features`, `gf_mean`, `norm_features`,\n  `x_grn_scaled`, `x_normalized`, `x_ln`, `x_expanded`, `x_gelu`) directly — do not recompute from\n  scratch, or rounding will drift from the reference.\n- **Output dtypes/shapes** must match exactly, including keep-dim shapes `grad_grn_weight/bias (1,1,1,C4)`\n  and `grad_pwconv2_bias (C,)`.\n- **Immutability**: one source version per candidate id; new id for any source/config/launch change.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Numerical risk register\n\n1. **TF32 vs IEEE fp32 in `tl.dot`.** `rtol = 1e-3` sits right at TF32's (~2⁻¹⁰ mantissa) error edge.\n   Accumulation is fp32 regardless, but TF32 truncates multiply operands. For large-magnitude reduction\n   outputs the wide `atol` likely absorbs it, but `grad_x` / LN-derived grads have modest magnitude where\n   `rtol` bites. **Plan:** start with `input_precision=\"ieee\"` (true fp32) for all `tl.dot` to guarantee\n   correctness; later test `\"tf32\"` as a *speed* candidate and keep it only if all 5 workloads still pass.\n2. **Non-standard `gf_mean` gradient (§2.5).** Copying the textbook (summed) version will fail the\n   `grad_grn_*` / `grad_x` chain. Replicate the element-wise, unsummed form exactly.\n3. **GELU tanh approx.** Reuse the exact constants and the *single* `tanh` evaluation reused in `cdf`\n   and `pdf`; recomputing/reordering changes low bits. Use `tl.math.tanh` (or `libdevice.tanh`) in fp32.\n4. **Divisions by (stat+eps).** `1/std`, `1/(gf_mean+eps)`, `1/(global_features+eps)`. `global_features`\n   is an L2 norm ≥ 0; `+eps` guards zero. Use given values, not recomputed norms.\n5. **Depthwise stencil boundaries (pad 3).** Off-by-one / flip errors in input-grad and weight-grad are\n   the highest-probability functional bug (see §2.12 cross-check). Mask out-of-range taps to 0.\n6. **Reduction associativity / atomics.** `grad_dwconv_weight`, biases, LN/GRN weight&bias sum over many\n   elements. Atomic-add or multi-block split changes summation order → last-bit noise, acceptable under\n   `atol`/`rtol`+98% match, but prefer deterministic (single-pass or tree) reductions where cheap to keep\n   headroom. Accumulate in fp32.\n7. **Match ratio 0.98.** Up to 2% of elements per tensor may miss; still aim for ~exact so no tensor\n   drifts systematically (a wrong constant would fail *all* elements, which 0.98 does not save).\n8. **Large-M accumulation error** (W3, M≈50k) for `grad_pwconv*_weight` (K=M reduction). fp32 accumulate;\n   consider split-K with fp32 partials if a single accumulator loses precision (unlikely to matter given\n   atol 5.2 on W3).\n9. **Empty/degenerate tiles**: masks for M not divisible by BLOCK, C4=512/C=128 are clean multiples of\n   common block sizes (32/64/128), which simplifies masking on the channel axes.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Where the reference is slow (optimization opportunity)\n\nThe reference `run(...)` has one catastrophic hotspot and several ordinary ops:\n\n- 🔴 **`for g in range(C): ...unfold...`** — a **Python loop over 128 channels**, each doing\n  `F.pad` + double `unfold` (materializing `(B,H,W,7,7)` patches) + multiply + 3-D sum. This is the\n  dominant cost, especially on W3 (128 × huge unfold), and launch/host overhead on small W1/W4. A single\n  fused Triton depthwise-weight-grad kernel replaces all 128 iterations → the primary speedup lever.\n- 🟠 4 GEMMs via cuBLAS (fast baseline; our Triton GEMM must be competitive, not just correct).\n- 🟠 `conv_transpose2d` via cuDNN for `grad_x` (fast baseline; depthwise stencil in Triton must match).\n- 🟢 Many element-wise/reduction ops materializing full `(M,C4)` temporaries repeatedly → **fusion**\n  reduces DRAM traffic (memory-bound).\n\n**Thesis:** even a *modular, correct* Triton port should beat the reference purely by eliminating the\n128-iteration Python loop; further gains come from (a) a well-tuned depthwise weight-grad kernel and\n(b) fusing the element-wise chains into the GEMM epilogues / stencil prologues.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Triton design space\n\nPipeline temporaries kept in **NHWC-flat `(M, ·)`** contiguous buffers so GEMMs are simple row-major:\n`grad_x_projected (M,C)`, `grad_x_grn (M,C4)`, `grad_x_expanded (M,C4)`, `grad_x_ln (M,C)`,\n`grad_x_nhwc (M,C)`, `grad_x_dwconv (B,C,H,W)`.\n\n### 6.1 Kernel decomposition (baseline modular plan for c001)\n- **K1 — permute+drop-path+bias** (grid over (M tiles, C)): compute `grad_x_projected[b,h,w,c] =\n  grad_output[b,c,h,w]*drop_mask[b]/keep_prob`; accumulate `grad_pwconv2_bias[c]` (reduction over M).\n- **K2 — GEMM** `grad_x_grn = grad_x_projected @ pwconv2_weight` (M×C×C4), fp32 `tl.dot`.\n- **K3 — GEMM** `grad_pwconv2_weight = grad_x_projectedᵀ @ x_grn` (C×C4, K=M).\n- **K4 — GRN reduce (pass 1)**: per (b,c) spatial sums → `grad_norm_features (B,C4)`; global sums →\n  `grad_grn_weight (C4)`, `grad_grn_bias (C4)`. Then compute `grad_global_features (B,C4)` from\n  `grad_norm_features`, `gf_mean`, `global_features` (§2.5, non-standard).\n- **K5 — GRN apply + GELU (pass 2)**: `grad_x_gelu` (residual + scaled + L2-norm paths) then GELU-grad →\n  `grad_x_expanded (M,C4)`.\n- **K6 — GEMM** `grad_x_ln = grad_x_expanded @ pwconv1_weight` (M×C4×C).\n- **K7 — GEMM** `grad_pwconv1_weight = grad_x_expandedᵀ @ x_ln` (C4×C, K=M); `grad_pwconv1_bias (C4)`\n  (reduce over M — can be epilogue of K5 or a small reduce).\n- **K8 — LN affine + normalization backward**: per-token (row) load C=128 channels, compute\n  `grad_layernorm_weight/bias` (reduce over M, accumulate) and `grad_x_nhwc (M,C)` via §2.9–2.10.\n  C=128 fits one row-block → single-pass row kernel (like Triton LN-bwd).\n- **K9 — depthwise input-grad stencil**: `grad_x = stencil(grad_x_nhwc→NCHW, W_flip) + grad_output`.\n- **K10 — depthwise weight-grad + bias**: `grad_dwconv_weight (C,49)` and `grad_dwconv_bias (C)` via\n  correlation of `grad_x_dwconv` with padded `residual`.\n\n### 6.2 Depthwise weight-grad options (highest-value kernel)\n- (a) **One program per channel**, serial grid-stride over `B×HW`, 49 fp32 register accumulators.\n  Simplest/deterministic; risk: single program serializes all M for a channel (W3 heavy).\n- (b) **Split over (channel, spatial-block)**, each program accumulates 49 partials for its block, then\n  `atomic_add` into `grad_dwconv_weight[c]` (or a `(nblk,C,49)` scratch + reduce kernel). Better\n  parallelism for W3; atomics add nondeterminism (tolerable).\n- (c) **Tile over channels in the inner axis** (vectorized 49-tap over a channel block) to raise ILP.\n  Start with (a) for c001 correctness; move to (b)/(c) for W3 throughput.\n\n### 6.3 Depthwise input-grad options\n- Per-`(b, c-block, spatial-tile)` stencil, 49 taps of flipped weight, boundary-masked, `+ grad_output`.\n  Channels contiguous (NCHW) → coalesced along W. Consider caching the 49 weights in registers/shared.\n\n### 6.4 GEMM tuning axes\n- Block sizes `(BM, BN, BK)`, `num_warps ∈ {4,8}`, `num_stages ∈ {2,3,4}`, `input_precision`\n  (`ieee` first, `tf32` later), grouped-M ordering for L2 reuse. K is 128/512/M.\n- **Fusion candidates**: fold K1's drop-path/permute into K2's A-load; fold `grad_pwconv2_bias` /\n  `grad_pwconv1_bias` into GEMM epilogues; fuse K4/K5 GELU into K6's A-load; fuse LN-affine into K8.\n  Autotune once shapes are known; C4=512, C=128 are TensorCore-friendly multiples.\n\n### 6.5 Persistence / occupancy\n- Reuse `(M,C4)` buffers across stages to cap DRAM. Small workloads (W1/W4) are launch-bound → minimize\n  kernel count / prefer fused kernels there; large W3 is compute/BW-bound → prioritize GEMM + weight-grad\n  throughput. A single set of launch params must serve all 5 (immutable per candidate), so autotune keys\n  on shape.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Implementation & candidate strategy (for plan.md later)\n\n- **c001 — correctness-first modular port.** All 10 kernels above, `input_precision=\"ieee\"`, deterministic\n  reductions, conservative block sizes. Goal: pass all 5 workloads; likely already > reference (kills the\n  Python loop). Establishes the correctness baseline and per-tensor sanity.\n- **c002+ — targeted speed** once green: (i) depthwise weight-grad option (b) split+atomic for W3;\n  (ii) GEMM autotune / grouped-M; (iii) fuse element-wise chains into GEMM epilogues & stencil prologues;\n  (iv) try `tf32` (guard: must keep all 5 passing); (v) reduce kernel count for W1/W4 launch overhead.\n- Change **one lever per candidate id**; re-evaluate; keep only monotone geomean improvements; record\n  parent/hash/hypothesis/per-workload/decision in `candidates.jsonl`.\n\n## 8. Validation strategy\n\n- **No local execution available** (Bash denied; no CUDA/profiler/nvidia-smi allowed). The *only* trusted\n  signal is `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` (5 workloads = 1 eval). Budget: 100 evals,\n  tokens soft 1.0M / hard 1.2M — so **spend evals deliberately**, not as a debugger.\n- **Pre-eval self-checks (by construction):** (1) shape/stride table for every kernel I/O matches §1.1\n  outputs; (2) reverse-order math matches §2 line-by-line, including the two non-standard steps; (3)\n  depthwise flip/offset cross-check (§2.12); (4) all `tl.dot` accumulate fp32; (5) no Torch compute path.\n- **Failure triage plan:** the evaluator reports per-workload pass/fail (and, we expect, which output\n  mismatches). Map failures to suspects — `grad_x`/`grad_dwconv_weight` → conv indexing; `grad_grn_*`/\n  `grad_x_gelu` → §2.5 non-standard step; broad small-magnitude misses on many tensors → TF32; single\n  tensor all-fail → a wrong constant/transpose.\n- **Metric:** geometric-mean speedup across the 5 feedback workloads, gated on every workload passing\n  correctness. Final 14-workload eval is operator-approval-only; never auto-run `final`.\n- **Convergence / stop:** stop when geomean plateaus across successive candidates, or on budget; then\n  write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` with the reason.\n\n## 9. Open questions to resolve during implementation\n- Exact evaluator match rule (assumed `|a-b| ≤ atol + rtol·|ref|`, ≥98% elements). Confirm empirically on c001.\n- Whether `tf32` passes `rtol=1e-3` on the small workloads (W1/W2/W4) — test as a dedicated candidate.\n- Best depthwise weight-grad parallelization for the wide range M∈[1568, 50176] under one immutable config.\n","numLines":285,"startLine":1,"totalLines":285}}}
